{
	"id": "g5.maths.divide_whole",
	"lesson": [
		{
			"text": "Quick memory: in grade 4 you met dividing with remainders. What is 17 ÷ 5? 3 remainder 2."
		},
		{
			"text": "768 ÷ 24 asks: how many 24s make 768? Start with multiples of 24 you know: 24 × 10 = 240, 24 × 20 = 480, 24 × 30 = 720."
		},
		{
			"text": "720 is 30 lots of 24. What is left? 768 − 720 = 48, and 48 = 2 × 24. So 768 ÷ 24 = 30 + 2 = 32.",
			"picture": {
				"kind": "area_model",
				"cols": [
					"30",
					"2"
				],
				"rows": [
					"24"
				],
				"cells": [
					[
						"720",
						"48"
					]
				]
			}
		},
		{
			"text": "Check by multiplying back: 32 × 24 = 768. If it does not match, look for the step that went wrong."
		},
		{
			"text": "Sometimes there is a remainder: 100 ÷ 24 = 4 remainder 4, because 24 × 4 = 96 and 100 − 96 = 4. In a story, think about what the remainder means: 100 children need 5 boats that hold 24 each.",
			"picture": {
				"kind": "bar_model",
				"bars": [
					{
						"parts": [
							{
								"text": "24",
								"size": 24
							},
							{
								"text": "24",
								"size": 24
							},
							{
								"text": "24",
								"size": 24
							},
							{
								"text": "24",
								"size": 24
							},
							{
								"text": "4",
								"size": 4
							}
						],
						"total": "100"
					}
				]
			}
		},
		{
			"text": "Try it in the world: you have 150 blocks to share equally between 12 friends. How many does each get, and how many are left? 12 each, 6 left. Next: adding and subtracting decimals."
		}
	],
